@page "/learn/async-form-validation"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Async validation · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Async validation</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Is this supplier code already taken? Only the database knows. Can your form wait for it?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> validation attributes can be async. <code>EditForm</code> waits for every check
        before it saves, and tells you while one is running. In .NET 10 validation was synchronous, so a database
        check had to live outside it, in code that Save didn't wait for.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form. A supplier code must look like <code>ABCD-01</code>, and it must not
        belong to another supplier. <code>EXOT-01</code> already belongs to Exotic Liquids.
    </p>
    <p>
        Only the database can answer the second rule, and here it takes 1.5 seconds, on purpose, like a busy ERP.
        This form checks the code the .NET 10 way: a handler that runs when the field changes.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The code check, in a field-changed handler" Url="demo/async-validation/suppliers/new/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: a field-changed handler" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            <p>
                <b>Try 1, a quick Save.</b> Type any company, type <code>EXOT-01</code> into <b>Supplier code</b>, and
                click <b>Save supplier</b> straight away, within a second. Save goes ahead: a red banner says
                validation passed and only the database's unique index stopped a second <code>EXOT-01</code>. About a
                second later, "already used" appears under the field, after Save has run.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Try 2, a quick change.</b> Press <b>Start over</b>. Type <code>EXOT-01</code> and press Tab, then,
                within a second, change it to <code>ZZZZ-99</code> and press Tab again. Wait two seconds: "already
                used" sits under <code>ZZZZ-99</code>, a code nobody has. It's the answer for <code>EXOT-01</code>.
            </p>
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Validation can't wait, so Save doesn't</h2>
    <p>Here's what happens when you leave the field and press Save straight away:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">One quick Save, in order</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">0.0 s</span>  leave Supplier code  lookup starts    <span class="dim">nobody awaits it</span>
<span class="dim">0.1 s</span>  press Save           attributes run   <span class="ok">✓ all pass</span>
<span class="dim">0.1 s</span>  OnValidSubmit        inserts EXOT-01  <span class="err">unique index throws</span>
<span class="dim">1.5 s</span>  lookup answers       "already used"   <span class="err">Save already ran</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 10, validation is synchronous. <code>EditForm</code> runs the attributes on the model, and none of
        them knows about the database. The lookup lives in an <code>async void</code> handler, so nothing can wait for it: not
        Save, and not the next edit. A second edit starts a second lookup, and the first one isn't cancelled. When it
        answers, it puts its message on whatever the field holds by then.
    </p>
    <p>
        In Fig. 1 a unique index happens to catch the duplicate, so what the user gets is a database exception in a
        banner, not a message on the field. A rule with no index behind it, like "this VAT number must be registered",
        has nothing to catch it: the bad value saves.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>An async attribute, with the other rules</h2>
    <p>Derive from <code>AsyncValidationAttribute</code>, and put the attribute on the property like any other.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">public sealed class UniqueSupplierCodeAttribute()
    : <span class="hl">AsyncValidationAttribute</span>("{0} is already used by another supplier.")
{
    protected override async Task&lt;ValidationResult?&gt; <span class="hl">IsValidAsync</span>(
        object? value, ValidationContext context, <span class="hl">CancellationToken cancellationToken</span>)
    { <span class="dim">... ask the database ...</span> }
}

[Required, RegularExpression("^[A-Z]{4}-[0-9]{2}$")]
<span class="hl">[UniqueSupplierCode]</span>
public string? Code { get; set; }

<span class="dim">// The page: redraw when a check ends</span>
<span class="hl">form.OnValidationStateChanged += (_, _) =&gt; InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);</span>

@@if (<span class="hl">form.IsValidationPending(() =&gt; Input.Code)</span>) { &lt;span&gt;Checking…&lt;/span&gt; }
&lt;button type="submit" disabled="<span class="hl">@@form.IsValidationPending()</span>"&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>EditForm</code> now awaits every check before it calls <code>OnValidSubmit</code>. A check finishes
        after the event that started it, so the page subscribes to <code>OnValidationStateChanged</code> to redraw
        when it does. Leave that line out and "Checking…" and the message wait for something else to redraw the
        page.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>IsValidationPending</code> comes in two forms. With a field, it's true while that field's check runs:
        that's the "Checking…" beside the box, after you Tab out. With no field, it's true only while Save is waiting
        for the whole form: that's what disables the button, from the moment you click it until every check has
        answered. Tabbing out of the field doesn't disable the button. Here's the same form, with the code check as an
        attribute.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, with an async attribute" Url="demo/async-validation/suppliers/new"
                Label=".NET 11: an async attribute">
        <Caption>
            <p>
                <b>Try 1 again.</b> A company, <code>EXOT-01</code>, and click <b>Save supplier</b> straight away. The
                button greys out and says <b>Checking…</b> for about a second and a half, then the field says
                "Supplier code is already used by another supplier." Nothing is saved, and there's no red banner.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Try 2 again.</b> <b>Start over</b>, type <code>EXOT-01</code>, Tab, and within a second change it to
                <code>ZZZZ-99</code> and Tab. "Checking…" shows beside the field, then clears with no message: the
                newer edit cancelled the check for <code>EXOT-01</code>.
            </p>
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the files behind both demos:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/UniqueSupplierCodeAttribute.cs" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The attribute Fig. 2 runs. It gets the database from the <code>ValidationContext</code>, and passes the
            token on, so a newer edit can cancel it.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/NewSupplierForm.cs" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The model: the async rule sits with the others, and only runs once they pass. Fig. 1 posts
            <code>NewSupplierFormNet10</code>, the same fields without it.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/NewSupplier.razor" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 2: "Checking…" beside the field while its check runs, and a Save button that waits for
            the whole form.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/NewSupplierNet10.razor" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 1: the same lookup, in a handler that nothing waits for.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>The check lived outside validation</h2>
    <p>A synchronous validator gave three places to put a database check, and each one cost something:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to check a code in .NET 10</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">async Task SaveAsync(EditContext form)
{
    if (<span class="hl">await IsTakenAsync(Input.Code)</span>)
    { messages.Add(...); return; }</pre>
                <span class="why">check again in the save handler</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the rule lives away from the others, and nobody hears about it until Save</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">form.OnFieldChanged += <span class="err">async</span> (_, e) =&gt;
{
    if (await IsTakenAsync(Input.Code))
        messages.Add(...);
};</pre>
                <span class="why">check in a field-changed handler, as Fig. 1 does</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ Save doesn't wait for it, and an old answer can land on a newer value</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">protected override ValidationResult? IsValid(...)
    =&gt; IsTakenAsync(code)<span class="err">.GetAwaiter().GetResult()</span> ...</pre>
                <span class="why">block on the lookup in a plain attribute</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ deadlocks the circuit: no message, and the form stops responding</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Fig. 1 runs the handler on its own. Most apps paired it with the save-handler check: the handler for early
        feedback, the save handler to catch what it missed. That closes the quick-Save hole in Try 1, but the stale
        answer in Try 2 stays, and the rule is now written twice, in the page, away from the attributes.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Validation that can await</h2>
    <p>
        <code>AsyncValidationAttribute</code> is new in <code>System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations</code>, and
        <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> runs it with the other rules: when the field changes, and again before
        Save. That's what Fig. 2 runs. The same attribute works on a static SSR form too: it runs when the form posts,
        and the post waits for it.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Name the field in the result.</b> Pass <code>[context.MemberName!]</code> to <code>ValidationResult</code>, as Fig. 3 does. Without it, the check Save runs files its message under no field: no <code>ValidationMessage</code> shows it, and the form just doesn't save. This demo's model is a plain class, so it needs this. A model marked <code>[ValidatableType]</code> (the source-generated validation from <a href="learn/validatable-type-stable">Nested validation</a>) gets the field filled in for it.</li>
        <li><b>Override <code>IsValid</code> too.</b> It's abstract. Throw, as Fig. 3 does: Blazor only calls <code>IsValidAsync</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Calling <code>Validate()</code> yourself?</b> Switch to <code>await form.ValidateAsync()</code>. <code>EditContext.Validate()</code> is obsolete in .NET 11, and throws once an async check is registered.</li>
        <li><b>Faulted isn't invalid.</b> If a check throws, the field gets no message. <code>IsValidationFaulted()</code> is true instead, and the form won't submit, so show a banner saying so.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
